00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.950
Speaker 1: I want to go over something sort
of in more detail that is really important

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.759
to understand because I'm now getting a ton
of emails about it.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:12.570
I want to devote its own segment to it rather
than just mentioning it as an aside.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:19.320
And what I want to talk about is the bogus
reporting in Florida of covid deaths that

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:25.840
no matter when you look, it looks like deaths
are declining so rapidly in Florida, things

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.539
must be going super well in Florida.

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:34.460
If you look at the death charts and Florida
has figured out a way to report deaths, that

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.910
it always creates a downward decline.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.210
Downward decline doesn't make a steep decline.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.420
And I want to explain to you exactly how it
works.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.199
There's actually an article about this in
the Miami Herald.

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:52.129
Florida changed its covid data, creating an
artificial decline in recent deaths.

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:55.710
And the article explains exactly how it works.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:02.210
As cases ballooned in August, the Florida
Department of Health changed the way it reported

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:08.050
death data to the CDC, giving the appearance
of a pandemic in decline.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.520
On Monday, Florida death data would have shown
would have shown an average of two hundred

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.640
and sixty two daily deaths over the previous
week under the former reporting system.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:26.150
Instead, the Monday update from Florida showed
just 46 new deaths per day over the previous

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.970
seven days.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.260
The dramatic difference is due to a small
change in the fine print.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.480
This is the key part.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.440
Until three weeks ago, data collected and
published on the CDC website counted deaths

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:45.130
by the day they were recorded a common method
for producing daily stats used by most states.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:51.860
On Aug. 10, Florida switched its methodology
and, along with just a handful of other states,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.600
began to tally new deaths by the date the
person died.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.690
If you chart deaths by Florida's new method
based on date of death, it will generally

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:06.280
appear even during a spike like the present,
that deaths are on a recent downslope.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.729
That's because it takes time for deaths to
be evaluated and death certificates to be

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:11.729
processed.

00:02:11.729 --> 00:02:17.390
When the deaths finally are tallied, they
are then assigned to the actual date of death,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:22.000
creating a spike where there was a downslope
in the past.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.570
This is let me paraphrase this.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.330
OK, here is what the chart of Florida deaths
look like.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.110
And you see that absolutely massive downslope.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:41.070
What is happening is if it takes longer than
seven days for a death to show up in the totals,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.940
it's not included in the current seven day
average.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:52.010
So when you delay assigning the deaths to
a specific day, at least a week, when you

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.360
look at the last seven days, there's almost
no deaths there.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.960
And that causes this massive decline.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.040
Current deaths, recent deaths will always
look low because it's taking you a week or

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.060
more to assign to those deaths to a date.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.630
This is extraordinarily disingenuous.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.180
And when you look at the seven day average,
it looks as though they're over the peak.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.380
They're seeing this big decline.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.660
And then on day eight, you assign the deaths
today.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.459
One, they're now outside of the seven day
average.

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:30.180
You look back at that day that said 40 deaths.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.560
Now it says two hundred and sixty or whatever
the number was.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.460
And you're constantly pushing the spike back
a week in time.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.780
Extraordinarily disingenuous.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.880
This should come as a surprise to no one who
is outside of the right wing news bubble.

